where we use a hatched blob to denote the exact $\sigma$ propagator. Specifically, it has been shown that interactions with light weakly coupled noncompact scalars always cause $\sigma$ to decay faster than any free field can [37, 38, 39], with $\gamma>\frac{1}{2}$ . That is, the light field provides an additional channel into which $\sigma$ can decay and it can decay faster and faster the lighter $\varphi$ is, though the perturbative expansion is quickly invalidated as $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ . This also prompts a small issue: if our universe is full of hundreds of light weakly coupled scalars, would the interactions with this horde not force $\gamma\gg 1$ and cause the cosmological collider signal (1.4) to decay too quickly to be observed?

We explain how to compute the corrections to $\sigma$ ’s long-distance behavior, and thus its observable cosmological collider signal, from light and massless compact scalar fields. By careful analytic continuation from Euclidean to Lorentzian signature, we find that there are qualitative differences between the signals generated by compact and noncompact scalars, and so the field space’s topology is a critical ingredient in determining the physics of light fields in de Sitter.

Specifically, we show that a light compact field $\varphi$ ’s effect on $\sigma$ depends sensitively on the ratio of the physical circumference of $\varphi$ ’s field space $2\pi f$ to the Hubble scale $H$ , where $f$ is often called the axion decay constant. In the noncompact limit, $2\pi f\gg H$ , we show that $\varphi$ generates corrections somewhat similar to those of a light noncompact scalar, allowing $\sigma$ to decay faster than any free field. However, unlike the noncompact scalar, these corrections are well-behaved and finite in the massless limit, as the compact field space regulates any large fluctuations.

The largest qualitative differences between compact and noncompact fields arise when the circumference $2\pi f$ is smaller than $H$ . Since the Hubble scale during inflation could have been as high as $H\sim\mathcal{O}\big(10^{14}\,{\mathrm{GeV}}\big)$ , this corresponds to a wide range of theoretically reasonable [15] decay constants. As we shrink the field space to be smaller than Hubble, $2\pi f/H\to 0$ , there is an interesting sort of resonant effect and we find that the correction to $\sigma$ ’s decay rate begins to oscillate in sign, enabling $\sigma$ to decay either faster or slower than any free field. Furthermore, we find that the correction to $\gamma$ is exponentially suppressed as $H/(2\pi f)\to\infty$ , and so $\sigma$ can interact with a throng of light compact fields and still be relatively long-lived as long as those fields have small enough field spaces. Furthermore, this signal from the compact scalar is largest when $2\pi f\sim H$ . Interestingly, axions with decay constants $f\lesssim\mathcal{O}(10^{14}\,{\mathrm{GeV}})$ are generic in stringy examples [15], and so this regime is plausibly phenomenologically relevant.

Outline In Section 2, we review the basic properties of free quantum field theory in de Sitter space, describing how to analytically continue any de Sitter-invariant two-point function from Euclidean to Lorentzian signature via the Watson-Sommerfeld transform and its inverse, the Lorentzian inversion formula. We then review how free massive noncompact fields behave in de Sitter space and describe their propagators.

In Section 3, we review how the gauge symmetry $\varphi\sim\varphi+2\pi$ is implemented at the level of the path integral measure. We do so first in the simplest system where this arises: the particle on the circle. We explain how this gauge symmetry is implemented by summing over “winding” trajectories and restricting the zero mode integral to its fundamental domain, $\varphi_{0}\in[0,2\pi)$ . We then extend this to compact scalar fields in Minkowski space, and explain why the field space topology is so difficult to detect there.

In Section 4, we describe massless compact scalar fields in de Sitter space. While correlation functions like $\langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\rangle$ are ill-behaved and diverge at long distances, we show that gauge-invariant correlation functions of the vertex operators $\mathcal{V}(x)\propto{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\varphi(x)}$ are both well-defined and well-behaved. In particular, we study the Bunch-Davies vertex propagator $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle$ in both position and momentum space, and show that is appropriately well-behaved at long distances.

In Section 5, we compute the correction to the long-distance behavior of a heavy field $\sigma$ in the presence of a light compact field $\varphi$ , in a simple toy model that mimics an axion interacting with a saxion. The compactness of $\varphi$ requires that we perturb in the aforementioned vertex operators, and there we address many of the subtleties that arise.

Finally, we present our conclusions in Section 6. In Appendix A we provide definitions of and relations between the various special functions used throughout the main text. Finally, in Appendix B we compute the corrected long-distance behavior of the vertex two-point function in the model of Section 5.

## 2 de Sitter Quantum Field Theory

In this section, we review the basic aspects of free quantum field theory in de Sitter space that are relevant for the later sections. Specifically, in §2.1 we describe the geometry of de Sitter space in both Euclidean and Lorentzian signatures. We then describe how to analytically continue de Sitter-invariant two-point functions from one signature to the other. In §2.2, we describe the momentum space representation of these two-point functions, reviewing the inversion formula that determines the representation appropriate for analytic continuation to Lorentzian signature. Finally, in §2.3, we review the basic properties of free massive scalar fields in de Sitter space.

### 2.1 de Sitter in Position Space

de Sitter space is the unique maximally symmetric spacetime with constant positive curvature. In $D=d+1$ spacetime dimensions, its metric is given by [55, 56]

$$ \mathrm{d}s^{2}=\ell^{2}\left[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\mathrm{d}t^{2}+\cosh^{2}t\,\mathrm{d}\Omega_{d}\right] $$

in so-called global coordinates, where $\mathrm{d}\Omega_{d}^{2}=\mathrm{d}\theta_{1}^{2}+\sin^{2}\theta_{1}\,\mathrm{d}\Omega_{d-1}^{2}$ is the standard round metric on the $d$ -dimensional sphere, with $\theta_{1},\ldots,\theta_{d-2}\in[0,\pi]$ and $\theta_{d-1}\in[0,2\pi)$ , while the global time $t\in\mathbb{R}$ . The radius of curvature $\ell$ is related to the Hubble constant $H=\ell^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ . Throughout, we will find it extremely convenient to work in Hubble units $H=1$ , such that all quantities are dimensionless and measured in units of Hubble. Dimensions can be restored in any expression by restoring appropriate powers of $\ell$ or $H$ . Furthermore, we will work in arbitrary spacetime dimension $D=2\alpha+1$ , only taking $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ at the very end of any calculation.

We want to study interacting quantum field theories of compact scalars in Lorentzian de Sitter space. However, it will be helpful both conceptually and technically to define these theories in Lorentzian signature via analytic continuation from Euclidean de Sitter space. Euclidean de Sitter space is also known as the sphere ${\mathrm{S}}^{D}$ , whose metric is the standard round metric

$$ \mathrm{d}s^{2}=g_{\mu\nu}\mathrm{d}x^{\mu}\,\mathrm{d}x^{\nu}=\mathrm{d}\Omega_{D}^{2}=\mathrm{d}\tau^{2}+\sin^{2}\tau\,\mathrm{d}\Omega_{d}^{2}\,, $$

with $\tau\in[0,\pi]$ . We may parameterize an arbitrary point $x$ on this $(d+1)$ -dimensional sphere via its $\tau$ coordinate and a unit vector ${\mathbf{x}}$ on the $d$ -dimensional sub-sphere, $x=(\tau,{\mathbf{x}})$ , where $|{\mathbf{x}}|^{2}=1$ .

Perturbation theory in Euclidean signature converges to define an interacting ${\mathrm{SO}}(D+1)$ -invariant state as long as the linearized field theory admits an ${\mathrm{SO}}(D+1)$ -invariant propagator, and so interacting Euclidean correlators will be both ${\mathrm{SO}}(D+1)$ -invariant (or covariant) and satisfy the Euclidean Schwinger-Dyson equations. We may then analytically continue these correlation functions to Lorentzian signature by taking $\tau\to it+\frac{\pi}{2}$ , combined with an appropriate $i\epsilon$ -prescription to select a particular operator ordering. These Lorentzian correlation functions will automatically be invariant under the de Sitter isometry group and satisfy the Lorentzian Schwinger-Dyson equations, thus defining a consistent de Sitter-invariant state. Technically, this way of defining the theory is useful because Euclidean de Sitter space has finite volume and thus any potential IR divergences are automatically regulated. Conceptually, it is useful because we will find that it is easiest to distinguish between the path integral measures of compact and noncompact scalars in Euclidean de Sitter space.

We will study the two-point functions of local operators in de Sitter space. Any function of two points on the sphere that is invariant under its isometry group, and thus continues to a Lorentzian de Sitter-invariant two-point function, may be parameterized via the embedding distance,

$$ \xi_{12}\equiv\xi(x_{1},x_{2})=\cos\tau_{1}\,\cos\tau_{2}+\sin\tau_{1}\,\sin\tau_{2}\,({\mathbf{x}}_{1}\cdot{\mathbf{x}}_{2})\,, $$

where ${\mathbf{x}}_{1}\cdot{\mathbf{x}}_{2}$ is the standard dot product in $\mathbb{R}^{d}$ . We will often drop the subscript on $\xi_{12}\to\xi$ when there is no risk of ambiguity. Geometrically, $\xi$ can be understood as the cosine of the angle subtended by the great arc connecting the two points if we embed the ${\mathrm{S}}^{D}$ into $\mathbb{R}^{D+1}$ . Clearly, in Euclidean signature it must lie in the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ . Upon analytic continuation to Lorentzian signature, $\tau_{i}\to it_{i}+\frac{\pi}{2}$ , it may take values on the entire real line $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$ . For instance, if the two points are connected via a spacelike geodesic, $\xi$ will still lie on the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ , while $\xi>1$ if they are connected by a timelike geodesic. If the two points are either coincident $x_{1}\to x_{2}$ or separated by a null geodesic, $\xi=1$ . Finally, $\xi<{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1$ if the two points cannot be connected by any geodesic. This last case is particularly important for cosmological observations, as taking two points to future infinity with fixed spatial separation sends $\xi\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\infty$ .

Many of our expressions, e.g. (2.13), will be more naturally written in terms of the variable

$$ \zeta=\frac{2}{\xi-1}\,, $$

in which the limits $\xi\to\pm\infty$ correspond to $\zeta\to 0^{\pm}$ . Because it is related to the long-time or long-distance limit, we will refer to the region $|\zeta|\in[0,1)$ as the “infrared,” while we will call the region $|\zeta|\in[1,\infty)$ the “ultraviolet.”

### 2.2 de Sitter in Momentum Space

A very useful feature of Euclidean de Sitter space is that there exists a useful momentum space representation tied to the de Sitter isometry group. We may decompose any scalar field into the hyperspherical harmonics $Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x)$ ,

$$ \sigma(x)=\sum_{{\mathbf{J}}}\sigma_{{\mathbf{J}}}Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x)\,. $$

These harmonics are the $D$ -dimensional analogs of the familiar spherical harmonics, which provide a complete and orthogonal basis of scalar functions on the sphere ${\mathrm{S}}^{D}$ ,

$$ \int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}\Omega_{D}\,Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x)\hskip 0.5pt{Y^{\raisebox{1.0pt}{$\scriptstyle*$}}}_{\hskip-7.0pt{\mathbf{K}}}(x)=\delta_{{\mathbf{J}}{\mathbf{K}}}\quad\text{and}\quad\sum_{{\mathbf{J}}}Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x){Y^{\raisebox{1.0pt}{$\scriptstyle*$}}}_{\hskip-7.0pt{\mathbf{J}}}(y)=\delta^{(D)}(x-y)/\sqrt{g}\,, $$

so that $\sigma_{{\mathbf{J}}}=\int\!\mathrm{d}\Omega_{D}\,\sigma(x){Y^{\raisebox{1.0pt}{$\scriptstyle*$}}}_{\hskip-7.0pt{\mathbf{J}}}(x)$ . These harmonics are labeled by a vector ${\mathbf{J}}=(J,m_{1},\ldots,m_{d})$ with $J\in\mathbb{N}$ a non-negative integer which we will call the total angular momentum and ${\mathbf{m}}=(m_{1},m_{2},\ldots,m_{d})\in\mathbb{Z}^{d}$ are a set of integers such that $J\geq m_{1}\geq m_{2}\geq\cdots\geq|m_{d}|$ which call the magnetic quantum numbers. Most importantly, these harmonics are eigenfunctions of the Laplacian on ${\mathrm{S}}^{D}$ ,

$$ \nabla^{2}Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x)=-J(J+2\alpha)Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x)\,, $$

whose eigenvalue only depends on total angular momentum $J$ . We will not need explicit forms for these harmonics, though a special role will be played by the zero mode with ${\mathbf{J}}=0$ ,

$$ Y_{0}(x)=\sqrt{\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{{\mathrm{vol}}\,{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}}\,, $$

which is just the constant profile on the sphere.

Any scalar function $H(x,y)$ of two points on the sphere also admits a decomposition in terms of these hyperspherical harmonics, which greatly simplifies whenever $H(x,y)=H(\xi)$ is invariant under the de Sitter isometry group. In this case, the momentum space representation of $H(x,y)$ , which we denote $[H]_{{\mathbf{J}}}$ , only depends on the total angular momentum $[H]_{\mathbf{J}}=[H]_{J}$ . Using

$$ \sum_{{\mathbf{m}}}Y_{J{\mathbf{m}}}(x){Y^{\raisebox{1.0pt}{$\scriptstyle*$}}}_{\hskip-7.0ptJ{\mathbf{m}}}(y)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}(J+\alpha)C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

we see that any de Sitter-invariant two-point function can be expanded in terms of the Gegenbauer polynomials $C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)$ ,

$$ H(\xi)=\sum_{{\mathbf{J}}}[H]_{J}Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x){Y^{\raisebox{1.0pt}{$\scriptstyle*$}}}_{\hskip-7.0pt{\mathbf{J}}}(y)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\sum_{J=0}^{\infty}(J+\alpha)[H]_{J}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

which are a complete set of orthogonal polynomials defined by (A.8) on the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ .

Given a function $H(\xi)$ , we may extract its momentum space representation $[H]_{J}$ for nonnegative integer $J$ via the Euclidean inversion formula,

$$ [H]_{J}=\frac{(4\pi)^{\alpha}\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}\int_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}^{1}\!\mathrm{d}\xi\,\big(1-\xi^{2}\big)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)H(\xi)\,. $$

Strictly speaking, the expansion (2.10) only converges in $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ when the inversion formula (2.11) does, and vice versa. The Gegenbauer polynomials can faithfully represent any smooth function on the interval as long as it is not too singular at the endpoints. Likewise, since $C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)\sim J^{2\alpha-1}$ as $J\to\infty$ , cf. (A.11), the expansion (2.10) only converges if $[H]_{J}$ decays faster than $J^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(2\alpha+1)}$ as $J\to\infty$ . This will not be true for the functions we work with when $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ . We will instead keep $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}(3-\epsilon)$ arbitrary throughout and use dimensional regularization to remove any $\epsilon$ -divergences, defining $H(\xi)$ and $[H]_{J}$ via analytic continuation in $\alpha$ .

Unfortunately, the expansion (2.10) is rather useless if we want to evaluate the function $H(\xi)$ at Lorentzian separations $|\xi|\gg 1$ , as Gegenbauer polynomial expansions are only guaranteed to converge along the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ . We instead define the analytic continuation of (2.10) to arbitrary $\xi$ via the Watson-Sommerfeld transform [57, 58, 59, 60, 39]

$$ H(\xi)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha}}\!\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}J}{2\pi i}\frac{(J+\alpha)[H]_{J}}{\sin\pi J}C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)\,, $$

which expresses the sum as an integral over a contour $\gamma$ which lies slightly to the left and runs parallel to the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J$ -axis. Here, $[H]_{J}$ is a function on the complex $J$ -plane which agrees with the coefficients appearing in (2.10) at the nonnegative integers. More precisely, the $[H]_{J}$ appearing in (2.12) is an interpolation of the coefficients appearing in (2.10), though we will not distinguish between the two notationally. Crucially, for (2.12) to both converge for arbitrary $\xi$ and to agree with the original sum (2.10) on the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ , the interpolation $[H]_{J}$ must be well-behaved and decay as $|J|\to\infty$ in the right-half $J$ -plane.

Given a function $H(\xi)$ defined for all $\xi\in\mathbb{C}$ , how then do we invert (2.12) and find its well-behaved momentum space representation $[H]_{J}$ ? Since the Gegenbauer polynomials grow exponentially away from the ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J$ -axis, $C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)\propto\exp(\pi|\hskip 0.5pt{\mathrm{Im}}\,J\hskip 0.5pt|)$ , the representation provided by (2.11) does not work. Instead, we must rely on the Froissart-Gribov formula [57, 58, 59, 60, 61, 39]

$$ [H]_{J}=\frac{(4\pi)^{\alpha}\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+1)}{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}\oint_{\mathcal{C}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\xi}{2\pi i}\,\big(\xi^{2}-1\big)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}Q_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)H(\xi)\,. $$

where $\mathcal{C}$ is a contour that wraps the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ counterclockwise, while the $Q_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)$ are the Gegenbauer $Q$ -functions defined in (A.12). This provides the unique extension of the coefficients appearing in (2.10) from nonnegative integers to all $J\in\mathbb{C}$ (save for potential singularities) that does not grow exponentially as $|J|\to\infty$ .

The two-point functions we work with will all have a discontinuity along $\xi\in[1,\infty)$ , or analogously $\zeta\in[0,\infty)$ , reflecting the fact that the ordering of time-like separated operators matters. We may thus deform the contour11 1 Technically, (2.13) only applies to functions which are analytic in a region surrounding the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ . This is not true for the functions we consider which all have a discontinuity along $\xi\in[1,\infty)$ . This is related to the fact that products of free propagators diverge more and more strongly in the coincident limit $\xi\to 1$ . This singular behavior strongly depends on $\alpha$ and so, in keeping with our general strategy, we will work at small enough $\alpha$ so that (2.14) applies and then define the $[H]_{J}$ at $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ by analytic continuation. Of course, $[H]_{J}$ may then diverge as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ , but these are the UV divergences one typically encounters in any loop calculation and may be absorbed by local counterterms. in (2.13) to run along this discontinuity,

$$ [H]_{J}=-\frac{2\pi^{\alpha+1}\Gamma(J+1)}{4^{J}\hskip 0.5pt\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\frac{\mathrm{d}\zeta}{2\pi i}\,\zeta^{J-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,{\mathrm{disc}}\,H(\zeta)\,, $$

with the discontinuity defined as

$$ {\mathrm{disc}}\,H(\zeta)=\lim_{\epsilon\to 0^{+}}H(\zeta+i\epsilon)-H(\zeta-i\epsilon)\,. $$

This Lorentzian inversion formula (2.14) is the main tool we will use in this work—it defines the momentum space representation of $H(\xi)$ as long as ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J$ is large enough and $\alpha$ is small enough so that the integral converges at its endpoints $\zeta\to 0$ and $\zeta\to\infty$ , respectively. Indeed, all of our loop calculations will eventually reduce to studying integrals of the form (2.14) with different choices of $H(\zeta)$ depending on which fields run in the loop.

### 2.3 Free Scalar Fields in de Sitter

Finally, we now review the properties of free fields in de Sitter space. A free, massive, minimally-coupled scalar field $\sigma$ in Euclidean de Sitter space, with Euclidean action

$$ S_{\textsc{e}}=\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}^{D}x\,\sqrt{g}\!\left[\tfrac{1}{2}(\partial\sigma)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}m^{2}\sigma^{2}\right], $$

has a propagator $G(x,y)$ which obeys the Klein-Gordon equation

$$ \big({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\nabla^{2}+m^{2}\big)G(x,y)=\delta^{(D)}(x-y)/\sqrt{g}\,, $$

where $\nabla^{2}\equiv g^{\mu\nu}\nabla_{\mu}\nabla_{\nu}$ and $\sqrt{g}$ are the Laplacian and the square-root of the metric (2.2)’s determinant, respectively. Expanding in hyperspherical harmonics (2.7), this can be solved to find

$$ G(x,y)=\sum_{{\mathbf{J}}}\frac{Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x){Y^{\raisebox{1.0pt}{$\scriptstyle*$}}}_{\hskip-7.0pt{\mathbf{J}}}(y)}{J(J+2\alpha)+m^{2}}=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\sum_{J=0}^{\infty}\frac{J+\alpha}{J(J+2\alpha)+m^{2}}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

which converges absolutely as long as $\xi$ lies in the Euclidean interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ .

The propagator in momentum space is thus

$$ [G]_{J}=\frac{1}{J(J+2\alpha)+m^{2}}=\frac{1}{(J+\Delta)(J+\bar{\Delta})}\,, $$

the poles of which are the so-called scaling dimension of the field,

$$ \Delta=\begin{cases}\alpha+i\sqrt{m^{2}-\alpha^{2}}\,,&m\geq\alpha\\
\alpha-\sqrt{\alpha^{2}-m^{2}}\,,&m<\alpha\end{cases} $$

and its conjugate or shadow dimension $\bar{\Delta}\equiv 2\alpha-\Delta$ . A “light” scalar field, with $m<\alpha$ , is said to belong to the complementary series with $\Delta\in(0,\alpha)$ . Likewise, a “heavy” scalar field, with $m\geq\alpha$ , is said to belong to the principal series with dimension $\Delta=\alpha+i\nu$ , $\nu\in\mathbb{R}$ .22 2 There are other representations, but these are the only two which will concern us.

Harmonic expansions like (2.18) only converge for spacelike separations $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ but, as we discussed in the previous section, we can analytically continue it to arbitrary Lorentzian $\xi\in\mathbb{R}$ via the Watson-Sommerfeld transformation (2.12) which, when applied to (2.19), yields

$$ G(\xi)=\frac{\Gamma(\Delta)\Gamma(\bar{\Delta})}{(4\pi)^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}\Gamma\big(\alpha+\frac{1}{2}\big)}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\big(\Delta,\bar{\Delta};{\alpha+\tfrac{1}{2}};\tfrac{1}{2}(1+\xi)\big)\,. $$

This free propagator is analytic for all $\xi$ aside from a branch cut along $\xi\in[1,\infty)$ or, in terms of our $\zeta$ variable (2.4), $\zeta\in[0,\infty)$ . It will also be helpful to use (A.4) to rewrite the propagator as

$$ G(\zeta)=\mathcal{G}_{\Delta}(\zeta)+\mathcal{G}_{\bar{\Delta}}(\zeta)=\mathcal{A}(\Delta)({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1/\zeta)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\Delta}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta, \,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \Delta- 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]+(\Delta\to\bar{\Delta})\,, $$

where we define the coefficient

$$ \mathcal{A}(\Delta)=\frac{1}{(4\pi)^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}}\frac{\Gamma(\Delta)\Gamma(2\alpha-2\Delta)}{\Gamma\big(\alpha+\frac{1}{2}-\Delta\big)}\,. $$

From this expression, we can see that the dimension $\Delta$ and its shadow $\bar{\Delta}$ , and thus the field’s mass $m$ , controls the asymptotic behavior of the free propagator for very large separations $\zeta\to 0$ or as $\xi\to\pm\infty$ . At long distances, heavy fields oscillate at a “rest mass” frequency set by $\nu=\sqrt{m^{2}-\alpha^{2}}$ and decay at a rate determined entirely by the expansion of spacetime, ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\Delta=\alpha$ , independent of their mass. In contrast, light fields decay at a rate set by their mass and do not oscillate.

Finally, this free field propagator diverges in the short distance or coincident limit $\xi\to 1$ in physical spatial dimensions $\alpha>\frac{1}{2}$ . However, since it will be useful for our loop calculations we may formally define this coincident limit via analytic continuation from $\alpha<\frac{1}{2}$ , in which

$$ \displaystyle G(1) \displaystyle=\frac{1}{(4\pi)^{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} - \alpha\,,\, \Delta\,,\, 2\alpha- \Delta$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\frac{1}{2} +\alpha- \Delta\,,\, \frac{1}{2} -\alpha+ \Delta$}}\right]\,. $$

As $\epsilon\to 0$ with $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}(3-\epsilon)$ , this behaves as

$$ G(1)\sim-\frac{(\Delta-1)(\bar{\Delta}-1)}{16\pi^{2}}\left[\frac{2}{\epsilon}-\psi\big(\Delta-1\big)-\psi\big(\bar{\Delta}-1\big)+\log 4\pi{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\gamma_{\textsc{e}}}+1\right]-\frac{1}{8\pi^{2}}\,, $$

keeping the mass of the field held fixed.33 3 It may be more natural to keep, say, the asymptotic behavior of the field fixed $\Delta$ held fixed as we send $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ , or its frequency $\nu={\mathrm{Im}}\,\Delta$ . In either case, this choice just changes the last term of (2.25) and reflects a choice of renormalization scheme.

Having reviewed the basics of both de Sitter space and its free scalar fields, we will now review the basic formulation of compact scalar fields at the level of the path integral. A compact scalar field $\varphi$ enjoys a gauge symmetry in which $\varphi$ and $\varphi+2\pi$ are identical—they are the same point in field space. Appropriately summing over field configurations that respect this identification requires that we modify the path integral measure and it will be convenient to first understand how this is done in flat space before moving onto de Sitter in Section 4.

## 3 Compact Scalar Fields in Flat Space

The goal of this section is to review how compact scalar fields differ quantum mechanically from noncompact scalar fields in Minkowski space. We will focus on how the path integral over field configurations must be modified to correctly account for the field space’s compactness and why it is difficult, without observing topological defects like axion strings, to distinguish between noncompact and compact scalar fields in Minkowski space. It will be clarifying to first study the simplest compact scalar field theory: the compact boson in $(0+1)$ spacetime dimensions, also known as the quantum mechanical particle on a circle. We will then extend this discussion to the compact boson in $(d+1)$ spacetime dimensions, in preparation for our discussion in Section 4 on the massless compact boson in de Sitter space.

### 3.1 The Particle on the Circle

We begin by reviewing the simplest compact scalar field theory: the quantum mechanical particle on a circle or the rotor. This theory is defined by the Lagrangian and Hamiltonian

$$ \mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{2}f^{2}\dot{\varphi}^{2}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\mathcal{H}=\frac{p_{\varphi}^{2}}{2f^{2}}\,, $$

where we take $\varphi\sim\varphi+2\pi$ to be an angle with $2\pi$ -periodicity whose canonical momentum is $p_{\varphi}=f^{2}\dot{\varphi}$ . The decay constant $f$ controls how damped fluctuations in $\varphi$ are. If this system describes a particle of mass $m$ on a circle with radius $R$ , then $f^{2}=mR^{2}$ is its moment of inertia. We have assumed that this particle has no potential energy, though this is only for calculational simplicity and none of our conclusions rely on this assumption. It will be helpful to review this simple system both from the canonical and path integral pictures to help calibrate our ruleset for quantization.

Canonically, the Hilbert space of (3.1) is given by the space of square-integrable functions on the circle $L^{2}({\mathrm{S}}^{1})$ . It is convenient to introduce a “position” operator $\hat{\varphi}$ with a complete set of eigenstates $\hat{\varphi}|\varphi\rangle=\varphi|\varphi\rangle$ that resolves the identity via

$$ \mathbbm{1}=\int_{0}^{2\pi}\!\mathrm{d}\varphi\,|\varphi\rangle\langle\varphi|\,, $$

where we integrate over a single “fundamental domain” of the circle, $\varphi\in[0,2\pi)$ .44 4 It is a gauge choice to take $\varphi\in[0,2\pi)$ and we could have instead worked with different covering of the circle, for example $\varphi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\pi,\pi)$ or $\varphi\in[2023\pi,2025\pi)$ . Of course, no physical results will depend on this choice. Crucially, $\hat{\varphi}$ is not a well-defined operator [62] on the Hilbert space since it does not map $L^{2}({\mathrm{S}}^{1})$ into itself, generally taking a single-valued function on ${\mathrm{S}}^{1}$ into a multi-valued one. Said differently, $\hat{\varphi}$ is not a gauge-invariant operator. As consequence, any physical state $|\Psi\rangle$ must be single-valued in this $|\varphi\rangle$ -basis and thus must obey $\langle\varphi+2\pi|\Psi\rangle=\langle\varphi|\Psi\rangle$ .

Similarly, expectation values like $\langle\Psi|\hat{\varphi}|\Psi\rangle$ , which we would normally use to measure the position of the particle, are completely nonsensical and will lead to unphysical results. Instead, we must consider expectation values of the gauge-invariant “vertex operators” $\langle\Psi|{{\mathrm{e}}}^{ip\hat{\varphi}}|\Psi\rangle$ , with $p\in\mathbb{Z}$ , to characterize the state $|\Psi\rangle$ . As illustrated in Figure 2, the phase and magnitude of $\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\hat{\varphi}}\rangle$ characterize where and how delocalized the particle is on the circle, respectively. If $\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\hat{\varphi}}\rangle=0$ , the particle is completely delocalized along the circle.

*Figure 2: The expectation value of the vertex operator $\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\hat{\varphi}}\rangle$ measures both the particle’s position and its spread. Roughly, the position of the particle on the circle is given by ${\mathrm{arg}}\,\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\hat{\varphi}}\rangle$ while the magnitude measures its variance, $|\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\hat{\varphi}}\rangle|\approx 1-\frac{1}{2}\Delta\varphi^{2}+\mathcal{O}\big(\Delta\varphi^{4}\big)$ . As such, this magnitude is close to 1 when the wavefunction is well-localized [left], and close to 0 if it is not [right].*

The Hamiltonian (3.1) has a discrete set of energy and momentum eigenstates,

$$ \langle\varphi|n\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{in\varphi}\,, $$

with energy and momentum eigenvalues $E_{n}=n^{2}/(2f^{2})$ and $p_{n}=n$ , respectively, for $n\in\mathbb{Z}$ . These also form an orthonormal $\langle n|k\rangle=\delta_{nk}$ and complete basis $\mathbbm{1}=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}|n\rangle\langle n|$ . Because it is simple to calculate using the Euclidean path integral, we will focus on the thermal trace

$$ \mathcal{Z}(\beta)\equiv{\mathrm{tr}}\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta\mathcal{H}}=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta E_{n}}\,. $$

This can also be derived from the Euclidean propagator,

$$ \langle\varphi|{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta\mathcal{H}}|\varphi^{\prime}\rangle=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}\langle\varphi|n\rangle\langle n|{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta\mathcal{H}}|n\rangle\langle n|\varphi^{\prime}\rangle=\frac{1}{2\pi}\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta E_{n}+in(\varphi-\varphi^{\prime})} $$

in which case

$$ \mathcal{Z}(\beta)=\int_{0}^{2\pi}\!\mathrm{d}\varphi\,\langle\varphi|{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta\mathcal{H}}|\varphi\rangle=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{Z}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta E_{n}}\,. $$

We emphasize that, for (3.4) and (3.6) to agree, the trace in “position space” (3.6) must only integrate over a single fundamental domain of the circle, $\varphi\in[0,2\pi)$ .

As is well known, we can also calculate (3.4) via the Euclidean path integral,

$$ \mathcal{Z}(\beta)=\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}(\tau)\,\exp\!\left(-\int_{0}^{\beta}\!\mathrm{d}\tau\,\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}\dot{\varphi}^{2}\right) $$

on the thermal circle, $\tau\sim\tau+\beta$ , where we impose periodic boundary conditions $\varphi(\tau)\sim\varphi(\tau+\beta)$ . Here, we use $\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}$ to denote that we integrate over periodic paths $\varphi(\tau)$ from the Euclidean time circle $\tau\sim\tau+\beta$ to the field space circle $\varphi\sim\varphi+2\pi$ . Since $\pi_{1}({\mathrm{S}}^{1})\in\mathbb{Z}$ , these paths are characterized by a winding number $\ell$ , which represents the number of times the path $\varphi(\tau)$ winds around field space as $\tau$ goes from $0$ to $\beta$ . An arbitrary such path may be parameterized as

$$ \varphi_{\ell}(\tau)=\varphi_{0}+2\pi\ell\tau/\beta+\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\big[a_{m}\cos\omega_{m}\tau+b_{m}\sin\omega_{m}\tau\big]\,, $$

where we have introduced the Matsubara frequencies $\omega_{m}=2\pi m/\beta$ and allowed $\varphi_{\ell}(\tau)$ to take values along the entire real line, $\varphi_{\ell}(\tau)\in\mathbb{R}$ . We have also introduced the two sets of Fourier coefficients $a_{m}$ , $b_{m}\in\mathbb{R}$ , and the average value of the path along the fundamental domain,

$$ \varphi_{0}=\frac{1}{\beta}\int_{0}^{\beta}\!\mathrm{d}\tau\,\left[\varphi_{\ell}(\tau)-2\pi\ell\tau/\beta\right]\,, $$

which must take values in $\varphi_{0}\in[0,2\pi)$ in order to avoid overcounting paths. This is a common trope: we write a path integral over a compact degree of freedom $\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}(\tau)$ in terms of one over a noncompact degree of freedom $\varphi(\tau)$ by including a sum over topological sectors or windings, here labeled by the integer $\ell$ .

Since the path integral (3.7) is Gaussian, we may evaluate it directly. Specifically, in terms of the path (3.8), we have

$$ \mathcal{Z}(\beta)=\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb{Z}}\int_{0}^{2\pi}\!\frac{\mathrm{d}\varphi_{0}}{C_{0}}\left[\prod_{m=1}^{\infty}\int_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\infty}^{\infty}\!\frac{\mathrm{d}a_{m}\,\mathrm{d}b_{m}}{C_{m}}\right]\exp\!\left(-\frac{(2\pi\ell f)^{2}}{2\beta}-\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{4}f^{2}\omega_{m}^{2}(a_{m}^{2}+b_{m}^{2})\right) $$

with the standard constants $C_{m}=4\pi/(\omega_{m}^{2}f^{2})$ and $C_{0}=\sqrt{2\pi\beta/f^{2}}$ that normalize the path integral measure [63]. Evaluating the Gaussian integrals, we arrive at

$$ \mathcal{Z}(\beta)=\sqrt{\frac{2\pi f^{2}}{\beta}}\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb{Z}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\frac{1}{2}\ell^{2}f^{2}/\beta} $$

which equals the original expression (3.4) upon Poisson resummation. As in (3.6), that the integral over the zero mode of the compact field is constrained to be in $\varphi_{0}\in[0,2\pi)$ is necessary to arrive at an answer that agrees with the canonical picture (3.4). Furthermore, introducing a potential to (3.1) will not change how the path integral is constructed, but will merely alter how the different paths are weighted.

To recap, we construct the thermal Euclidean path integral for a compact scalar field by integrating over all unique paths that come back to themselves. Since the field space is a circle, these paths can wind $\ell$ times around it yet still arrive at the same point, and so we must also include a sum over windings or topological sectors in our definition of the path integral measure. Furthermore, the field space’s compactness restricts the zero mode of these paths $\varphi_{0}$ to lie in the fundamental domain $\varphi_{0}\in[0,2\pi)$ , lest we overcount. In Section 4, we will find that this latter constraint fundamentally changes the behavior of compact scalars in de Sitter as compared to noncompact scalars. Before we move on, however, it will be useful to understand why this difference is unimportant for the compact scalar in $(d+1)$ -dimensional Minkowski space.

### 3.2 Compact Scalar Field Theory

Let us now consider the higher-dimensional generalization of the particle on a circle, the compact scalar field in $(D=d+1)$ -dimensional flat space, with Euclidean action

$$ S_{\textsc{e}}=\int\!\mathrm{d}^{D}x\,\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}(\partial\varphi)^{2} $$

and $\varphi(x)\sim\varphi(x)+2\pi$ . We will again study this theory, and its differences from the noncompact scalar with the same action, by computing the thermal trace. This would involve evaluating the Euclidean path integral on the cylinder ${\mathrm{S}}_{\beta}^{1}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}$ with periodic Euclidean time, $x^{0}=\tau\sim\tau+\beta$ . However, to tame infrared divergences, it will be more convenient to also compactify the spatial directions $x^{i}\sim x^{i}+L$ and consider the theory on the $D$ -dimensional torus ${\mathrm{T}}^{D}$ with spatial side lengths $L$ . The goal of this section is to show that the dynamics that distinguish the compact scalar from the noncompact one freeze out in the limit of infinite volume, $L\to\infty$ , and so it is impossible to distinguish between the two in Minkowski space from the dynamics of (3.12) alone.55 5 As mentioned in the introduction, this does not preclude distinguishing the two by observing finite energy topological defects like axion strings, whose existence relies on the compactness of the field space.

We are instructed to integrate over all single-valued field configurations on the torus ${\mathrm{T}}^{D}$ . As in the previous section, the scalar field may shift by a multiple of $2\pi$ as we wind around the thermal circle,

$$ \varphi(\tau+\beta,{\mathbf{x}})=\varphi(\tau,{\mathbf{x}})+2\pi\ell\,, $$

with $\ell\in\mathbb{Z}$ . However, the field may also wind in the spatial directions,

$$ \varphi(\tau,x^{1},\ldots,x^{k}+L,\ldots,x^{d})=\varphi(\tau,{\mathbf{x}})+2\pi w_{k}\,, $$

and so we must include $d$ additional winding numbers $w_{i}\in\mathbb{Z}$ . So, any path will be characterized by a set of winding numbers $(\ell,{\mathbf{w}})\in\mathbb{Z}^{D}$ which describe how many times the field $\varphi$ winds around its field space as we move around each of the $D$ nontrivial cycles in the ${\mathrm{T}}^{D}$ . This is in contrast to the noncompact scalar field, with the same action (3.12), which cannot wind and still respect the periodic boundary conditions.

We may write the most general map from ${\mathrm{T}}^{D}\to{\mathrm{S}}^{1}$ as

$$ \varphi(x)=\varphi_{\ell}(\tau)+2\pi w_{i}x^{i}/L+\tilde{\varphi}(x)\,, $$

where we have split off the field’s spatially isotropic zero mode

$$ \varphi_{\ell}(\tau)=\varphi_{0}+2\pi\ell\tau/\beta+\sum_{m\neq 0}\big[a_{m}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\omega_{m}\tau}+{\mathrm{c.c.}}\big] $$

and its non-zero mode

$$ \tilde{\varphi}(x)=\frac{1}{L^{d/2}}\sum_{\omega_{m},k_{i}\neq 0}\big[a_{\omega_{m},k_{i}}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\omega_{m}\tau+ik_{i}x^{i}}+{\mathrm{c.c.}}\big]\,, $$

where the sum is over all Matsubara frequencies $\omega_{m}=2\pi m/\beta$ and non-zero spatial momenta $k_{i}\in 2\pi\mathbb{Z}/L$ , and the Fourier coefficients are arbitrary complex numbers, $a_{m}\in\mathbb{C}$ and $a_{\omega_{m},k_{i}}\in\mathbb{C}$ . The zero mode (3.16) is equivalent to the particle on the circle (3.8), and describes how the spatially constant mode on the torus ${\mathrm{T}}^{d}$ evolves in time $\tau$ . As before, the average value of this mode $\varphi_{0}$ is constrained to $\varphi_{0}\in[0,2\pi)$ , though our discussion here will not center on this.

The Euclidean action (3.12) evaluated on the configurations (3.15) reduces to

$$ S_{\textsc{e}}[\varphi_{0},\tilde{\varphi}]=\tfrac{1}{2}L^{d}f^{2}\int_{0}^{\beta}\!\mathrm{d}\tau\,\dot{\varphi}_{\ell}^{2}+4\pi^{2}\beta L^{d-2}\sum_{i=1}^{d}w^{2}_{i}+\int\!\mathrm{d}^{D}x\,\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}(\partial\tilde{\varphi})^{2}\,. $$

In the limit $L\to\infty$ , we see that for $d\geq 3$ , the different winding sectors $w_{i}\neq 0$ acquire infinite action, and so the sum over windings localizes to the ${\mathbf{w}}=0$ sector. Furthermore, fluctuations in the zero mode—including the $\ell\neq 0$ winding trajectories—completely freeze out. That is, in the limit $L\to\infty$ , it is completely consistent to consider states in which the field’s zero mode is localized at a definite position in its field space. In the limit $L\to\infty$ , the theory’s dynamics will not delocalize the zero mode, and we can consider it to be a non-dynamical parameter of the theory. Indeed, this is often what we do for scalar fields or moduli in Minkowski space, where we quantize the field about a specific vacuum expectation value $\langle\varphi\rangle=\varphi_{0}$ , integrating over fluctuations $\tilde{\varphi}$ which decay at spatial infinity $|{\mathbf{x}}|\to\infty$ . The infinite weight of space ensures that states (or theories) with different expectation values $\langle\varphi\rangle$ are in superselection sectors.

We conclude that, since the only modes that “see” the compactness of the scalar field space are completely frozen out in the infinite volume limit, we cannot distinguish between a noncompact and compact scalar field in Minkowski space in $D=4$ , without relying on physics beyond what is described by (3.12). Said differently, it is impossible to detect the topology of the field space in Minkowski space without observing finite energy objects that are charged under $\varphi\to\varphi+2\pi$ . In $D=3$ and $D=4$ , these objects are vortices and axion strings, respectively. As we discuss in the next section, however, de Sitter space is different—compact and noncompact scalar fields can have completely different predictions, even if they are described by the same action.

## 4 Massless Free Compact Scalars in de Sitter

The sharpest difference between compact and noncompact scalars in de Sitter space is for massless fields. Said simply, the massless compact scalar exists while its noncompact counterpart does not. In this section, we will discuss the free massless compact scalar field in arbitrary $D$ -dimensional de Sitter space. We first describe its Euclidean path integral and physical correlation functions. We then discuss their analytic continuation to Lorentzian signature in both position and momentum space. We will use these results in Section 5, where we describe how a heavy field $\sigma$ propagates in the presence of this massless compact scalar and its effect on $\sigma$ ’s cosmological collider signal.

### 4.1 The Euclidean Path Integral

The theory of a massless compact scalar in de Sitter is defined by the Euclidean path integral

$$ \mathcal{Z}=\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}\,\exp\left(-\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\!\mathrm{d}^{D}x\,\sqrt{g}\,\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}(\partial\varphi)^{2}\right), $$

where $\varphi(x)\sim\varphi(x)+2\pi$ . Here, the decay constant $f$ is measured in units of Hubble and is thus dimensionless. As in Section 3, we use $\mathcal{D}\varphi_{c}$ to denote the integration over compact field configurations. It will be convenient to expand $\varphi(x)$ into its hyperspherical harmonics,

$$ \varphi(x)=\varphi_{0}+\tilde{\varphi}(x)=\varphi_{0}+\sum_{{\mathbf{J}}\neq 0}\varphi_{{\mathbf{J}}}Y_{{\mathbf{J}}}(x)\,, $$

explicitly separating out the Euclidean zero mode $\varphi_{0}$ and denoting the non-zero modes as $\tilde{\varphi}(x)$ .

Recall that to evaluate the path integral over compact field configurations, we could instead integrate over noncompact field configurations at the cost of including a sum over winding configurations and restricting the zero mode integral in $\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}$ to the field space’s fundamental domain. Unlike the particle on the circle, here $\pi_{D}({\mathrm{S}}^{1})=0$ is trivial for $D\geq 2$ and so there are no smooth topologically non-trivial maps from Euclidean de Sitter space ${\mathrm{S}}^{D}$ into $\varphi$ ’s field space ${\mathrm{S}}^{1}$ . Any smooth field configuration $\varphi(x):{\mathrm{S}}^{D}\to{\mathrm{S}}^{1}$ may be generated by an appropriate choice of the coefficients $\varphi_{{\mathbf{J}}}$ . Thus, the only effect the field space’s compactness has on the path integral (4.1) is to restrict the integral over the zero mode $\varphi_{0}$ to the fundamental domain of the circle [64], $\varphi_{0}\in[0,2\pi)$ , while the non-zero mode fluctuations $\tilde{\varphi}(x)$ are treated identically to those of the noncompact scalar field described in Section 2.66 6 This is not to say that the sum over windings is trivial for all questions in de Sitter space. For example, to compute the path integral on the hemisphere to find the Bunch-Davies state, we need to include the sum over winding configurations. For equilibrium questions computed from (4.1), however, this sum trivializes.

We work in Euclidean de Sitter space because there it is obvious how to define the path integral measure for a compact scalar field. That is not the case in the flat slicing. In Poincaré coordinates, naively one expects that the scalar field’s zero mode $\varphi_{0}$ sees an infinite spatial volume and freezes out in a way analogous to how it behaves in flat space. However, it is unclear whether it is more appropriate to regulate this spatial volume using the Hubble volume as suggested by Starobinsky’s stochastic picture of inflation [22, 23, 24]. Indeed, a Hubble-regulated spatial slice is more physically meaningful from the perspective of an observer living inside de Sitter space, as super-horizon modes are not observable. It is thus unclear exactly which modes in the flat slicing should “see” the compactness of $\varphi$ ’s field space. We sidestep this confusing conceptual question by instead working in Euclidean de Sitter space, where the answer is obvious.

Since the Euclidean action in (4.1) only depends on the non-zero modes,

$$ S_{\textsc{e}}[\tilde{\varphi}]=\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\!\mathrm{d}^{D}x\,\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}(\partial\varphi)^{2}=\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}\sum_{{\mathbf{J}}\neq 0}J(J+2\alpha)|\varphi_{{\mathbf{J}}}|^{2}\,, $$

the Euclidean zero mode is completely unconstrained absent a potential for $\varphi$ . In this massless limit, the Bunch-Davies state distributes the Euclidean zero mode uniformly along $\varphi$ ’s field space [25]. As illustrated in Figure 1, this is disastrous for the noncompact scalar field. Writing the propagator (2.18) in the limit $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ as

$$ \langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\rangle=\langle\varphi_{0}^{2}\rangle+\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{f^{2}}\sum_{J=1}^{\infty}\frac{J+\alpha}{J(J+2\alpha)}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

the variance of the zero mode $\langle\varphi_{0}^{2}\rangle$ for a noncompact scalar field,

$$ \langle\varphi_{0}^{2}\rangle=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{m_{\varphi}^{2}f^{2}}\, $$

diverges in the massless limit $m_{\varphi}\to 0$ , rendering the propagator and this theory ill-defined. For the compact scalar field, however, $\varphi_{0}$ is constrained to lie in $[0,2\pi)$ , and so the variance of the zero mode cannot diverge but instead approaches a constant

$$ \langle\varphi_{0}^{2}\rangle=\frac{4\pi^{2}}{3}\,, $$

so that (4.4) is well-defined. Physically, this makes sense. Absent a potential, $\varphi$ will approach its equilibrium state by random walking and will eventually explore its entire field space. For the noncompact field, this leads to a diverging variance since the field can walk arbitrarily far away from $\varphi=0$ without penalty. The compact field cannot walk arbitrarily far because there is no “arbitrarily far.” The equilibrium state simply fills in the circle, as illustrated in Figure 1, and the variance is finite.

There have been attempts to render the massless scalar field in de Sitter consistent by either explicitly projecting out this divergent zero mode [41, 42] or by restricting to shift-symmetric observables like $\langle\big(\varphi(x)-\varphi(y)\big)^{2}\rangle$ [41, 43] so that any dependence on the ill-behaved zero mode cancels out. We see that the compact scalar field, already so ubiquitous in beyond the Standard Model physics, automatically regulates this divergent zero mode.

We will specifically be interested in the non-zero mode two-point function [41, 42],

$$ \tilde{G}(\xi)=\langle\tilde{\varphi}(x)\tilde{\varphi}(y)\rangle=2\beta\sum_{J=1}^{\infty}\frac{J+\alpha}{J(J+2\alpha)}C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\,, $$

where, for convenience, we have defined the constant

$$ \beta\equiv\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{4\pi^{\alpha+1}}\frac{1}{f^{2}}\,. $$

For example, when $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ , this is just half the square of the ratio between the Hubble scale and the circumference of the field space, $\beta=\frac{1}{2}(2\pi f)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}$ . We may analytically continue (4.7) to arbitrary $\xi$ by taking the massless limit of the normal propagator (2.21) without the zero mode,

$$ \beta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\tilde{G}(\xi)=\lim_{\Delta\to 0}\left[\frac{\Gamma(\Delta)\Gamma(\bar{\Delta})}{\Gamma(2\alpha)}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta, \,\, \bar{\Delta}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha+ \tfrac{1}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{1+\xi}{2}\,\right]-\frac{2\alpha}{\Delta\bar{\Delta}}\right]. $$

This limit can be explicitly evaluated to yield

$$ \langle\tilde{\varphi}(x)\tilde{\varphi}(y)\rangle=\tilde{G}(\xi)=\frac{2\alpha\beta}{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}\left(\frac{1+\xi}{2}\right){}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$1,\, 1,\, 2 \alpha+ 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2,\, \alpha+ \frac{3}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{1+\xi}{2}\,\right]-\beta H_{2\alpha}\,, $$

where $H_{n}$ is the $n$ ’th harmonic number, defined in Appendix A. In three spatial dimensions, $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ , this reduces to

$$ \tilde{G}(\xi)=\frac{1}{16\pi^{2}f^{2}}\left[\frac{2}{1-\xi}+2\log\left(\frac{2}{1-\xi}\right)-\frac{14}{3}\right]. $$

Unfortunately, we see that even though the zero mode has been regulated by the compact field space, the massless scalar propagator diverges logarithmically in the infrared. This is true in arbitrary dimensions as well, since in the coordinate (2.4) the non-zero mode two-point function behaves as

$$ \beta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\tilde{G}(\zeta)\sim-\log\big({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1/\zeta\big)+\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi}}\Gamma\big(\alpha+\tfrac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\alpha)({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2/\zeta)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2\alpha}-\beta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\tilde{G}_{0} $$

as $\zeta\to 0$ or $|\xi|\to\infty$ , where we have defined the constant

$$ \beta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\tilde{G}_{0}=H_{2\alpha}+H_{2\alpha-1}-H_{(2\alpha-1)/2}\, $$

for later convenience.

That this propagator does not decay in the infrared is extremely problematic if we want to study interacting theories with a massless compact scalar, since it is not clear whether such interacting theories are even perturbatively stable in the infrared unless internal propagators decay at large Lorentzian separations, $\xi\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\infty$ [65]. Furthermore, even if the interacting theory is perturbatively stable, this poor infrared behavior can wreak havoc on the loop expansion as generally there are an infinite class of diagrams—beyond just the usual one-particle irreducible ones—that must be resummed to calculate a physically sensible answer in Lorentzian signature.

Fortunately, the two-point function $\langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\rangle$ is neither gauge-invariant nor physical, and so it is not a problem if it does not decay as $|\xi|\to\infty$ . Local operators $\varphi(x)$ are not gauge-invariant and so the usual $n$ -point functions like $\langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\rangle$ and $\langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\varphi(z)\rangle$ are nonsensical for the compact scalar. We should instead compute correlation functions of gauge-invariant operators, and in the next two subsections we will focus on the properties of the so-called vertex operators in both position and momentum space.

### 4.2 Vertex Propagator in Position Space

We will study the correlation functions of the vertex operators

$$ \mathcal{V}_{p}(x)\propto{{\mathrm{e}}}^{ip\varphi(x)}\,, $$

in the Bunch-Davies state prepared by the Euclidean path integral (4.1). These operators are clearly invariant under the gauge symmetry $\varphi\to\varphi+2\pi$ as long as the charge $p$ is an integer. We will suppress the subscript for the charge $p=1$ vertex operator, i.e. $\mathcal{V}(x)\equiv\mathcal{V}_{1}(x)$ , and use the conjugate to negate the charge, $\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}_{p}(x)\equiv\mathcal{V}_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}p}(x)$ . Of course, these are composite operators and so they must be defined through some sort of normal-ordering scheme, but we will find that—unlike the correlation functions of $\varphi(x)$ —the $n$ -point functions of these vertex operators do decay as $|\xi|\to\infty$ and thus they can be used to define a well-behaved perturbative expansion of an interacting field theory in de Sitter.

Let us first consider an arbitrary product of vertex operators which, up to an overall normalization, we may compute via

$$ \big\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\sum_{i}p_{i}\varphi(x_{i})}\big\rangle=\frac{1}{\mathcal{Z}}\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}S_{\textsc{e}}[\tilde{\varphi}]}\,\exp\!\Big[\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pti\varphi_{0}\big({\textstyle\sum_{i}}p_{i}\big)+i\big({\textstyle\sum_{i}}p_{i}\tilde{\varphi}(x_{i})\big)\Big]\,. $$

Since the action (4.3) does not depend on the zero mode, the integration over $\varphi_{0}$ forces the correlator to vanish unless the correlator is “charge neutral,”

$$ \big\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\sum_{i}p_{i}\varphi(x_{i})}\big\rangle\propto\int_{0}^{2\pi}\!\mathrm{d}\varphi_{0}\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\varphi_{0}\sum_{i}p_{i}}\neq 0\quad\text{iff}\quad\sum_{i}p_{i}=0\,. $$

If we add a potential for $\varphi(x)$ , this condition will be relaxed and correlation functions like $\langle e^{ip\varphi(x)}\rangle$ develop non-vanishing $x$ -independent expectation values whose phases roughly correspond to the minimum of the potential, cf. Figure 2.

In the massless theory, this charge neutrality condition forces the zero mode to drop out of correlation functions so that they only depend on shift-symmetric combinations like $\varphi(x)-\varphi(y)$ , similar to [41, 43]. Since the path integral is Gaussian, we can then evaluate an arbitrary vertex correlation function in terms of the non-zero mode propagator (4.10),

$$ \big\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\sum_{i}p_{i}\varphi(x_{i})}\big\rangle=\exp\!\left[-\tfrac{1}{2}{\textstyle\sum_{i,j}}p_{i}p_{j}\big\langle\tilde{\varphi}(x_{i})\tilde{\varphi}(x_{j})\big\rangle\right]. $$

For simplicity, let us consider the gauge-invariant analog of the two-point function,

$$ \langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle=\big\langle{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i(\varphi(x)-\varphi(y))}\big\rangle=\exp\!\Big[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\langle\tilde{\varphi}(x)\rangle^{2}+\langle\tilde{\varphi}(x)\tilde{\varphi}(y)\rangle\Big]\,. $$

Immediately, we can see that this correlator is ill-defined because it depends on the quantity

$$ \langle\tilde{\varphi}^{2}(x)\rangle=\tilde{G}(1)\equiv\beta\big(\pi\tan\pi\alpha-H_{2\alpha}\big)\sim\frac{1}{8\pi^{2}f^{2}}\left[\frac{2}{\epsilon}-\frac{11}{6}\right]\,, $$

which diverges as $\epsilon\to 0$ with $\alpha=\tfrac{1}{2}(3-\epsilon)$ and $\beta$ held fixed.77 7 A priori, it is unclear whether to keep $\beta$ or $f$ held fixed as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ . However, since we will find that it is $\beta$ that characterizes the physical behavior of the field over long distances and not $f$ , it is more natural to approach $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ holding $\beta$ fixed. This is a problem with all vertex correlators and we need to supply some notion of normal ordering to properly define the operator and its correlation functions. Thus, we will define the normal-ordered vertex operator to remove this infinite constant [66],

$$ \mathcal{V}_{p}(x)\equiv{:\mathrel{{{\mathrm{e}}}^{ip\varphi(x)}}:}\equiv\exp\!\left(\tfrac{1}{2}p^{2}\tilde{G}(1)+\tfrac{1}{2}\beta p^{2}\tilde{G}_{0}\right){{\mathrm{e}}}^{ip\varphi(x)}\,, $$

plus an additional constant to ensure that $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle$ is “canonically normalized” as $|\xi|\to\infty$ . Arbitrary $n$ -point functions of these vertex operators are then well-defined and given by

$$ \left\langle{\textstyle\prod_{i}}\,{:\mathrel{{{\mathrm{e}}}^{ip_{i}\varphi(x_{i})}}:}\right\rangle=\exp\!\left[\tfrac{1}{2}\tilde{G}_{0}\,{\textstyle\sum_{i}}p_{i}^{2}-{\textstyle\sum_{i>j}}p_{i}p_{j}\langle\tilde{\varphi}(x_{i})\tilde{\varphi}(x_{j})\rangle\right], $$

as long as the correlator is “neutral,” $\sum_{i}p_{i}=0$ , and vanishes otherwise. Specifically, the vertex two-point function is simply

$$ \mathcal{G}(\xi)\equiv\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle=\mathcal{A}\hskip 0.5pt\exp\!\left[\frac{2\alpha\beta}{\alpha+\frac{1}{2}}\left(\frac{1+\xi}{2}\right){}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$1,\, 1,\, 2 \alpha+ 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2,\, \alpha+ \frac{3}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{1+\xi}{2}\,\right]\right]\,. $$

with

$$ \mathcal{A}\equiv\exp\!\left(\hskip 0.5pt\beta H_{2\alpha-1}-\beta H_{(2\alpha-1)/{2}}\right). $$

The other two-point functions $\langle\mathcal{V}^{\phantom{\dagger}}_{p}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}_{p}(y)\rangle$ can be recovered by simply taking $\beta\to p^{2}\beta$ . Our goal for the remainder of this section is to study the two-point function (4.22) in both position and momentum space.

Unlike the non-zero mode two-point function (4.12), this vertex two-point function does decay at large separations,

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{G}(\xi)\sim\left({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{2}{\xi}\right)^{\beta}\left[1-\frac{1}{16}\frac{\beta}{\alpha-1}\left({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{2}{\xi}\right)^{2}-\mathcal{O}\big(\xi^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}4}\big)\right] \displaystyle-\frac{\beta\csc\pi\alpha\,\Gamma\big(\alpha+\frac{1}{2}\big)^{2}}{2\Gamma(2\alpha+1)}\left({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{2}{\xi}\right)^{2\alpha+\beta}\Big[1+\mathcal{O}\big(\xi^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}2}\big)\Big] $$

as $|\xi|\to\infty$ , with the dominant decay rates set by the two real exponents $\beta$ and $2\alpha+\beta$ . We should contrast this with a light scalar field, whose correlations also decay as $|\xi|\to\infty$ , $G(\xi)\sim\mathcal{C}_{\vphantom{\bar{\Delta}}\Delta}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\Delta}+\mathcal{C}_{\bar{\Delta}}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(2\alpha-\Delta)}$ with $\Delta\in(0,\alpha]$ . Not only can the dominant decay of the compact scalar be faster than a noncompact scalar, $\beta>\alpha$ , but the subleading asymptotic scaling is also different. Note that this is not in conflict with the representation theory of de Sitter space [67], as the vertex operator does not transform in an irreducible representation of the de Sitter isometry group—it is a composite operator.

As $\beta\to 0$ , we find that the vertex correlation function decays more slowly. Intuitively, this makes sense as this limit also corresponds to sending $f\to\infty$ and penalizing fluctuations in $\varphi$ more and more, forcing the compact field to take more time or distance to relax back to its vacuum. Likewise, the propagator decays more quickly in the ultracompact limit $\beta\to\infty$ , wherein $f\to 0$ so that $\varphi$ can fluctuate very easily and relax to its vacuum almost instantaneously.

Furthermore, it is clear from (4.21) that the vertex operators do not behave like Gaussian free fields, in the sense that the $n$ -point functions do not Wick factorize. Using (4.21), we may construct any charge neutral vertex $n$ -point function by forming the product of one vertex propagator $\mathcal{G}(\xi_{ij})$ for each pair of oppositely-charged operators and an inverse propagator $\mathcal{G}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}(\xi_{ij})$ for each pair of like-charged operators. For instance, the four-point function is

$$ \langle\mathcal{V}(x_{1})\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(x_{2})\mathcal{V}(x_{3})\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(x_{4})\rangle=\frac{\mathcal{G}(\xi_{12})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{14})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{23})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{34})}{\mathcal{G}(\xi_{13})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{24})}\neq\mathcal{G}(\xi_{12})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{34})+\mathcal{G}(\xi_{14})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{23})\,, $$

which clearly does not Wick factorize. However, if we take any two charge neutral pairs, say $(x_{1},x_{2})$ and $(x_{3},x_{4})$ , far apart from one another while keeping their constituents close together, e.g. $|\xi_{13}|,|\xi_{14}|,\ldots\gg|\xi_{12}|,|\xi_{34}|$ so that $\xi_{13}\approx\xi_{14}$ , then using (4.24) this correlator does factorize into a simple product

$$ \langle\mathcal{V}(x_{1})\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(x_{2})\mathcal{V}(x_{3})\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(x_{4})\rangle\to\mathcal{G}(\xi_{12})\mathcal{G}(\xi_{34})\,, $$

since each member of a charge neutral pair sees both the oppositely- and like-charged member of the other, producing a factor like $\mathcal{G}(\xi_{14})/\mathcal{G}(\xi_{13})$ that approaches $1$ as we send the pairs apart $|\xi_{14}|\sim|\xi_{13}|\to\infty$ . So, if we integrate over the $x_{i}$ in Lorentzian de Sitter space, there will be some regions of the integral in which the vertex operators do effectively Wick factorize. We will rely on this approximate factorization in the next section to identify higher-order contributions that dominate when an internal particle can go “on-shell.”

Finally, the vertex two-point function (4.22) is obviously regular at $\xi={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1$ but is extremely singular at coincident points,

$$ \mathcal{G}(\xi)\sim\mathcal{A}\exp\!\left(\frac{\beta\,2^{\frac{1}{2}-\alpha}\sqrt{\pi}\,\Gamma\big(\alpha-\frac{1}{2}\big)}{\Gamma(\alpha)(1-\xi)^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}}+\pi\beta\tan\pi\alpha\right)\,,\mathrlap{\qquad\xi\to 1\,,} $$

as we should expect from a propagator in the Bunch-Davies state. The vertex propagator is only regular as $\xi\to 1$ when $\alpha<\frac{1}{2}$ , while for $\alpha>\frac{1}{2}$ this is an essential singularity. In one spatial dimension, $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}$ , it is either a pole or branch point depending on the value of $\beta$ . Many of the manipulations we do, especially when we work with the momentum space representation of this propagator, are technically only well-defined when $\mathcal{G}(\xi)$ is not too singular along the Euclidean interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ . However, this usually does not matter in practice (even the free field propagator is strictly “too singular” when $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ ) but we will play it safe by keeping $\alpha$ arbitrary throughout and analytically continuing from $\alpha<\frac{1}{2}$ to $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ [39]. The singular nature of these functions then usually results in simple poles in $\epsilon$ , with $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}(3-\epsilon)$ , which must be subtracted via appropriate counterterms. However, somewhat magically, we will find that even though the vertex two-point function is extremely singular as $\xi\to 1$ , in loops it is extremely well-behaved in the ultraviolet.88 8 We note that a similar mechanism, in which UV divergences can be softened by including all of the interactions from a periodic potential, has appeared in [68].

### 4.3 Vertex Propagator in Momentum Space

It will also be useful, especially for our perturbative calculations, to compute the momentum space representation $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ of the vertex propagator. As discussed above, this is slightly awkward because expressions like

$$ \mathcal{G}(\xi)\equiv\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha+1}}\sum_{J=0}^{\infty}(J+\alpha)[\mathcal{G}]_{J}\,C^{\alpha}_{J}(\xi)\,, $$

and its inverse (2.11) do not converge for $\mathcal{G}(\xi)$ in physical dimensions, $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ , even for Euclidean separations $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ . We will ignore the Euclidean inversion formula and instead define $\mathcal{G}(\xi)$ ’s momentum space representation $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ for arbitrary $\alpha$ via the Lorentzian inversion formula (2.14),

$$ [\mathcal{G}]_{J}=-\frac{2\pi^{\alpha+1}\Gamma(J+1)}{4^{J}\hskip 0.5pt\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\frac{\mathrm{d}\zeta}{2\pi i}\,\zeta^{J-1}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\,{\mathrm{disc}}\,\mathcal{G}(\zeta)\,. $$

We will then confirm that, when used with the Watson-Sommerfeld transformation (2.12), this momentum space representation indeed captures the correct asymptotic behavior (4.24) of the position space propagator (4.22).

To compute the vertex propagator’s discontinuity along $\zeta\in[0,\infty)$ , it will be extremely helpful to use the connection formula (A.4) to rewrite the propagator as

$$ \displaystyle\beta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\log\mathcal{G}(\zeta)=\lim_{\Delta\to 0}\Bigg[ \displaystyle\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta\,,\, \bar{\Delta} - \Delta\,,\, \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \alpha\,,\, \alpha+ \frac{1}{2} - \Delta$}}\right]\left(-\frac{1}{\zeta}\right)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\Delta}\!\!{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\Delta, \,\, \Delta- \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\Delta- \bar{\Delta} + 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle+(\Delta\leftrightarrow\bar{\Delta})-\frac{2\alpha}{\Delta\bar{\Delta}}+2H_{2\alpha}-H_{(2\alpha-1)/2}-\frac{1}{2\alpha}\Bigg]\,. $$

On either side of the branch cut along $\zeta\in[0,\infty)$ , we then find that $\mathcal{G}(\zeta)$ behaves as

$$ \displaystyle\lim_{\epsilon\to 0^{+}}\beta^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}\log\mathcal{G}(\zeta\pm i\epsilon) \displaystyle=\log\zeta-\frac{1}{2}\zeta\,{}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$1,\, 1,\, \frac{3}{2} - \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2,\,2 - 2 \alpha$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] \displaystyle+\frac{{{\mathrm{e}}}^{\mp 2\pi i\alpha}}{2\sqrt{\pi}}\big(\zeta/2\big)^{2\alpha}\Gamma\big({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha+\tfrac{1}{2}\big){}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$2 \alpha, \,\, \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]\mp i\pi\,. $$

This expression will be especially helpful in the loop computations of Section 5, but here we can use it to derive the (rather intimidating) discontinuity

$$ {\mathrm{disc}}\,\mathcal{G}(\zeta)={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2\hskip 0.5pti\zeta^{\beta}e^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta\mathcal{F}(\zeta)}\sin\pi\beta\Phi(\zeta)\,, $$

where we have defined the functions

$$ \mathcal{F}(\zeta)=\frac{1}{2}\,\zeta\,{}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$1,\, 1,\, \frac{3}{2} - \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2,\, 2 - 2 \alpha$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]+\frac{\pi\cot 2\pi\alpha\,\Gamma\big(\alpha+\frac{1}{2}\big)\zeta^{2\alpha}}{\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(2\alpha+1\big)}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$2 \alpha, \,\, \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right] $$

and

$$ \displaystyle\Phi(\zeta) \displaystyle=1-\frac{\Gamma\big(\alpha+\frac{1}{2}\big)\zeta^{2\alpha}}{\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-\alpha\big)\Gamma\big(2\alpha+1\big)}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$2 \alpha, \,\, \alpha+ \frac{1}{2}$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]. $$

Even though this discontinuity is extremely complicated and we are not at present able to evaluate (4.29) exactly for arbitrary $\alpha$ , we can still extract essential physics from it using the techniques presented in [39] by appropriately expanding the integrand as a series in $\zeta$ .

In the deep ultraviolet, $\zeta\to\infty$ , the discontinuity (4.32) is shockingly well-behaved. There,

$$ \mathcal{F}(\zeta)\sim\frac{\Gamma\big(\alpha+\frac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma\big(\alpha-\frac{1}{2}\big)}{2\sin\pi\alpha\,\Gamma(2\alpha)}\,\zeta^{\alpha-\frac{1}{2}}\,,\mathrlap{\qquad\zeta\to\infty\,,} $$

so that the discontinuity exponentially decays, specifically behaving as $\propto{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta\zeta/2}$ as $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ . The ultraviolet part of the integral $\zeta\in[1,\infty)$ is thus completely regular and need not be regulated. We thus do not expect any $\epsilon$ -poles as we analytically continue from $\alpha<\frac{1}{2}$ to $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ . This is quite shocking when we consider the extremely poor behavior of $\mathcal{G}(\zeta)$ along any other direction in the complex plane. The point $\zeta\to\infty$ is an essential singularity for all $\alpha>\frac{1}{2}$ , and by Picard’s Great Theorem $\mathcal{G}(\zeta)$ takes on all possible complex values, with at most a single exception, infinitely many times in the punctured neighborhood around $\zeta=0$ [69]. If we approach the point $\zeta\to\infty$ ( $\xi\to 1$ ) from any direction other than along the positive real $\zeta$ line, then $\mathcal{G}(\zeta)$ and its discontinuity behave very poorly and are extraordinarily singular. Fortunately, as evidenced by the inversion formula (4.29), physics only seems to care about the behavior of $\mathcal{G}(\zeta)$ along where it is most well-behaved. Somewhat amazingly, loop corrections involving this compact scalar will also be UV finite, aside from a multiplicative renormalization that results from the normal ordering (4.20). Of course, there may still be ultraviolet divergences from other sources.

To confirm that this momentum space representation actually yields the propagator (4.22) when used with the Watson-Sommerfeld transform (2.12), it will be helpful to rewrite it using the recurrence relation (A.9) as

$$ \mathcal{G}(\xi)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha}}\!\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}J}{2\pi i}\,\big([\mathcal{G}]_{J}-[\mathcal{G}]_{J+2}\big)\csc\pi J\,C_{J}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)\,, $$

where the contour $\gamma$ again runs parallel along, but slightly to the left of, the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J$ -axis. As described in [37], since $C_{J}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)\sim\mathcal{C}_{1}(2\xi)^{J}+\mathcal{C}_{2}(2\xi)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(J+2\alpha+2)}$ , we extract (4.36)’s long-distance behavior by moving the contour $\gamma$ as far to the left as ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\alpha+1)$ , picking up any poles $\{J_{*}\}$ that we encounter along the way while still suppressing the magnitude of the integral as $|\xi|\to\infty$ ,

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{G}(\xi)= \displaystyle{\displaystyle\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha}}\sum_{\{J_{*}\}}\underset{{J=J_{*}}}{{\mathrm{res}}}\Big[\left([\mathcal{G}]_{J}-[\mathcal{G}]_{J+2}\right)\csc\pi J\,C_{J}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)\Big] \displaystyle+\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)}{2\pi^{\alpha}}\!\int_{\gamma^{\prime}}\frac{\mathrm{d}J}{2\pi i}\,\big([\mathcal{G}]_{J}-[\mathcal{G}]_{J+2}\big)\csc\pi J\,C_{J}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)\,. $$

The poles closest to the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J$ -axis thus determine the asymptotic behavior of (4.36), and we can use their residues to confirm that the momentum space representation $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ encodes the same asymptotic behavior (4.24) as the position space representation (4.22).

Following the techniques of [39], we can quickly extract the relevant singularities $\{J_{*}\}$ from the discontinuity (4.32). Since the integrand in (4.29) is extremely well-behaved as $\zeta\to\infty$ , the only way a $J$ -dependent singularity can develop in $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ is if the integrand diverges as $\zeta\to 0$ . We can thus read off the singularity structure of $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ by studying the discontinuity of $\mathcal{G}(\zeta)$ as $\zeta\to 0$ ,

$$ \displaystyle{\mathrm{disc}}\,\mathcal{G}(\zeta) \displaystyle\sim-2i\sin\pi\beta\,\zeta^{\beta}\left[1-\tfrac{1}{2}\beta\zeta+\tfrac{1}{16}\beta\big(2(1+\beta)+\tfrac{1}{1-\alpha}\big)\zeta^{2}+\cdots\right] \displaystyle-\frac{i\beta}{{2^{2\alpha}\sqrt{\pi}}}\sin(\pi\beta+2\pi\alpha)\Gamma\big(\alpha+\tfrac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\alpha)\zeta^{2\alpha+\beta}\Big[1-\tfrac{1}{2}(2\alpha+\beta)\zeta+\cdots\Big] \displaystyle-\frac{i\beta^{2}}{2^{4\alpha+2}\pi}\sin(\pi\beta+4\pi\alpha)\big[{\Gamma\big(\alpha+\tfrac{1}{2}\big)\Gamma({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\alpha)}\big]^{2}\zeta^{4\alpha+\beta}\Big[1-\tfrac{1}{2}(4\alpha+\beta)\zeta+\cdots\Big] \displaystyle+\mathcal{O}\big(\zeta^{\beta+6\alpha}\big)+\mathcal{O}\big(\zeta^{\beta+8\alpha}\big)+\cdots\,. $$

As $\zeta\to 0$ , the integrand of (4.29) will have terms that go as $\zeta^{J+\beta-1}$ , $\zeta^{J+\beta}$ , $\zeta^{J+\beta+2\alpha-1}$ , and so on, which will generate poles in $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ upon integration at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ , $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\beta+1)$ , $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\beta+2\alpha)$ , and so on, respectively. In total, $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ will potentially have poles at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}(\beta+2\alpha n+k)$ , for all positive integer $n$ and $k$ , though many of their residues may (and will) vanish. As $\alpha\to\frac{3}{2}$ from below, the poles closest to the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J$ -axis are

$$ \displaystyle{}_{J} \displaystyle\sim\frac{\pi^{\alpha}\Gamma(1-\beta)\sin\pi\beta}{2^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(2\beta+1)}\Gamma(\alpha-\beta+1)}\frac{1}{J+\beta}+\frac{\pi^{\alpha}\alpha\beta^{2}\Gamma(-\beta-1)\sin\pi\beta}{2^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(2\beta+1)}(\alpha-1)\Gamma(\alpha-\beta)}\frac{1}{J+\beta+2}+\cdots \displaystyle+\frac{\beta\,\sin(\pi\beta+2\pi\alpha)}{4^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(\alpha+\beta)}\pi^{\frac{1}{2}-\alpha}}\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}\,,\,\minus\alpha\,,\, 1 - \beta- 2 \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$1 - \beta- \alpha$}}\right]\frac{1}{J+\beta+2\alpha}+\cdots\,, $$

and so (4.37) reduces to

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{G}(\xi)= \displaystyle 4^{\beta}\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\alpha+ 1\,,\, 1 - \beta$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha- \beta+1$}}\right]C_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)+\frac{\beta}{2^{1-2\beta}}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$2 \alpha+ 1\,,\, \minus\alpha\,,\, 1-2\alpha- \beta$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$1 -\alpha- \beta$}}\right]C_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(\beta+2\alpha)}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi) \displaystyle-\frac{\beta(1-\alpha+\beta)(\alpha\beta-1)}{4^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta}(\alpha-1)}\,\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\alpha+1\,,\, -\beta- 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha- \beta+ 1$}}\right]C_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}(\beta+2)}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)+\cdots\,, $$

where the $\cdots$ denote terms subleading as $|\xi|\to\infty$ . Note that this no longer depends on the factor $\sin\pi\beta$ , and so this does not vanish as $\beta\to\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle>0}$ even if (4.38) does.99 9 It would be interesting to understand what happens at such special values of $\beta$ . Even at the free-field level, they seem special since the momentum space representation (4.32) vanishes there, $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}\propto\sin\pi\beta$ when $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ . We can understand this by noting that, for $\beta\in\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle>0}$ and $\alpha=\frac{3}{2}$ , the time- and anti-time-ordered two-point functions of the vertex operator are equivalent. This may be a flaw in our description—the position space propagator (4.22) certainly does not vanish, and again points to the need to keep $\alpha$ arbitrary until the very end of our calculations. On the other hand, there are other discrete values of $\beta$ that seem to be special for the self-energy of the vertex operators, presented in Appendix B. Furthermore, the analogous model in flat two-dimensional space—the Sine-Gordon model—has an interesting structure as one changes the equivalent of $\beta$ . So, perhaps the limit $\beta\to\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle>0}$ , where the decay constant $f$ and Hubble constant have commensurate values, is physically interesting. However, here we will simply assume that $\beta$ avoids these special values and instead leave their study for future work. This exactly recovers the large-distance expansion (4.24) of the position space propagator, and so we can have confidence that the inversion formula (4.29) with the discontinuity (4.32) defines the appropriate momentum space representation of the vertex two-point function $\mathcal{G}(\xi)$ .

This concludes our discussion of the free massless compact scalar field in de Sitter space. In the next section, we consider a theory with a light compact scalar field interacting with a heavy scalar $\sigma$ and compute how this interaction changes the long-distance behavior of $\sigma$ ’s two-point function, to leading order in perturbation theory.

## 5 Light Interacting Compact Scalars in de Sitter

In the previous section, we described the dynamics of free massless compact scalar fields in de Sitter space. There, we found that the two-point function of vertex operators was both well-defined and well-behaved, decaying at large Lorentzian separations $|\xi|\to\infty$ . In this section, we will study how a massless (or very light) compact scalar $\varphi$ affects the propagation of a heavy scalar $\sigma$ over long distances and thus modifies $\sigma$ ’s cosmological collider signal. Specifically, we will compute the self-energies of both $\varphi$ and $\sigma$ in the presence of a simple interaction and potential.

We will consider the theory defined by the Euclidean action

$$ S_{\textsc{e}}=\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}^{D}z\,\sqrt{g}\left[\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}(\partial\varphi)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}(\partial\sigma)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}m_{\sigma}^{2}\sigma^{2}+g_{\varphi}\cos\varphi+\tfrac{1}{2}g\sigma^{2}\cos\varphi\right]. $$

One can interpret this as, for instance, a toy model of a light axion $\varphi$ interacting with a heavy saxion $\sigma$ . Because the propagator $\langle\varphi(x)\varphi(y)\rangle$ does not decay at large distances, it is not useful to expand (5.1) in powers of $\varphi(x)$ . It is instead much more helpful to keep the vertex operator $\mathcal{V}(x)={:\mathrel{{{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\varphi(x)}}:}$ as a fundamental object, whose two-point function (4.22) does decay at large distances, and expand in terms of it. We may write the action (5.1) as the sum of a free part,

$$ S_{0}=\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}^{D}z\,\sqrt{g}\left[\tfrac{1}{2}f^{2}(\partial\varphi)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}(\partial\sigma)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}m_{\sigma}^{2}\sigma^{2}\right], $$

and an interaction part

$$ S_{\mathrm{int}}=\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}^{D}z\,\sqrt{g}\left[g_{\varphi}\mathcal{V}+\tfrac{1}{2}g\sigma^{2}\mathcal{V}+{\mathrm{c.c.}}\right], $$

where we have redefined the couplings $g$ and $g_{\varphi}$ to absorb the (divergent) normal-ordering constant (4.20) relating ${{\mathrm{e}}}^{i\varphi(x)}$ and $\mathcal{V}(x)$ , and a factor of two. We also include a counterterm action,

$$ S_{\mathrm{ct}}=\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}^{D}z\,\sqrt{g}\left[\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{Z_{\sigma}}(\partial\sigma)^{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{m_{\sigma}}\sigma^{2}+\big(\delta_{g_{\varphi}}\mathcal{V}+{\mathrm{c.c.}}\big)+\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{V}}\right], $$

where we omit the $\delta_{g}\sigma^{2}\cos\varphi$ counterterm because it will not contribute at leading order in perturbation theory and we will define the operator $\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{V}}$ below. We will allow the couplings and their associated counterterms to be complex, accounting for the two possible phases in the sinusoids. That is, we may shift the phase of $g_{\varphi}\cos\varphi\to g_{\varphi}\cos(\varphi+\delta)$ by taking $g_{\varphi}\to g_{\varphi}{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}i\delta}$ .

As we discussed in §4.2, the vertex operators $\mathcal{V}(x)$ do not behave as Gaussian free fields and this unfortunately complicates any diagrammatic expansion we might perform especially since there is no longer a complete cancellation of “disconnected” diagrams. We will instead explicitly expand out the path integral to compute the interacting one-point function $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\rangle$ and two-point functions $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle$ and $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle$ . However, it will still be useful (even if just from a psychological perspective) to represent these corrections diagrammatically, though we will not specify rules for their combinatorics. We will denote external $\sigma$ , $\mathcal{V}$ , $\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}$ operators using the black, blue, and red open circles, respectively,

