Unlike the other vertex interactions, we demand that $\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{V}}$ is such that it only connects two vertex propagators. Unfortunately, we do not know how to write $\mathcal{O}_{\mathcal{V}}$ in terms of the vertex operators themselves, but this does not affect the predictivity of the model---we are simply defining this operator in terms of its matrix elements with the vertex operators.1010 10 Composite operators are notorious for needing their own counterterms [70] which do not necessarily take a simple form in terms of the “fundamental” fields over which we path integrate. There are other computational schemes [71, 72] that do not rely on such counterterms to renormalize the theory, and perhaps this perturbative expansion in vertex operators is more natural in those languages. Indeed, we will mainly be interested in the corrected heavy field two-point function $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle$ at leading order in perturbation theory which only knows about the tree-level behavior of the vertex two-point function $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle\to\mathcal{G}(x,y)$ , and so our main results will be independent of precisely how we choose to renormalize the vertex two-point function. To that end, we will mainly relegate our discussion of the vertex two-point function to Appendix B.

Our goal now is to compute loop corrections to $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle$ and the vertex propagator $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle$ at leading order in perturbation theory. That is, we want to compute the self-energies $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)$ and $\Pi_{\varphi}(J)$ , which we define in terms of the connected two-point functions

$$ \langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha}}\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}J}{2\pi i}\frac{(J+\alpha)}{[G^{\sigma}]^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}_{J}-\Pi_{\sigma}(J)}\frac{C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)}{\sin\pi J}\, $$

and

$$ \langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha)}{2\pi^{\alpha}}\int_{\gamma}\frac{\mathrm{d}J}{2\pi i}\frac{(J+\alpha)}{[\mathcal{G}]^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}_{J}-\Pi_{\varphi}(J)}\frac{C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)}{\sin\pi J}\,. $$

As discussed in the Introduction, as long as $\sigma$ is not too heavy it will generate a decaying yet oscillatory cosmological collider signal in the primordial bispectrum, the frequency and decay rate of which are determined by the long-distance behavior of $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ . Using the techniques of [37] and §4.3, the long distance decay of $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}\propto\mathcal{C}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)^{J_{*}}+\bar{\mathcal{C}}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)^{\bar{J}_{*}}$ is determined by the poles $J_{*}$ and $\bar{J}_{*}$ closest to the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J$ -axis. Assuming that the self-energy is a small correction, the pole originally at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma}$ is shifted to

$$ J_{*}\approx-\!\left[\alpha+\frac{{\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})}{2\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}}\right]-i\!\left[\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}+\frac{{\mathrm{Re}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})}{2\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}}\right]\,, $$

while its shadow at $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ experiences the same (albeit complex conjugated) shift. The mass counterterm $\delta_{m_{\sigma}}$ can be used to ensure ${\mathrm{Re}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})=0$ so that $\nu_{\sigma}=\sqrt{m_{\sigma}^{2}-\alpha^{2}}$ corresponds to $\sigma$ ’s measured “rest mass frequency” [38], but the change in $\sigma$ ’s decay produced by a non-trivial ${\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})\neq 0$ is real and physical. Our main goal, then, is to compute the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})$ generated by $\sigma$ ’s interactions with a light compact scalar $\varphi$ .

As discussed [37, 38, 39] interactions with a light noncompact scalar field will always cause $\sigma$ to dilute faster than any free field in de Sitter space, ${\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}(\varphi)>0$ , providing an inflationary detection channel for these light fields. Interestingly, we find that interactions with a light compact scalar can be qualitatively different and depend sensitively on the circumference of the field space $2\pi f$ . In the noncompact limit $f\to\infty$ or $\beta\ll 1$ , we find that a compact scalar generates corrections very similar to those generated by a light noncompact scalar, enabling $\sigma$ to decay faster than any free field, ${\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})>0$ . However, as the circumference becomes smaller than Hubble $\beta\gg 1$ , we find that ${\mathrm{Im}}\,\Pi_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\Delta_{\sigma})$ begins to oscillate, causing $\sigma$ to decay either faster or slower than any free field.

To prevent our expressions from becoming overly long, we will introduce several abbreviations. We denote local fields evaluated at specific coordinates with a subscript, $\sigma_{i}\equiv\sigma(z_{i})$ , $\mathcal{V}_{i}\equiv\mathcal{V}(z_{i})$ , $G^{\sigma}_{ij}\equiv G^{\sigma}(z_{i},z_{j})$ , and $\mathcal{G}_{ij}=\mathcal{G}(z_{i},z_{j})$ . Here, the $z_{i}$ denote dummy variables over which we integrate, but we will also use $z_{x}=x$ and $z_{y}=y$ to denote the external coordinates. Furthermore, we use $\mathcal{Z}=\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}\,\mathcal{D}\sigma\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}S_{\textsc{e}}}$ and $\mathcal{Z}_{0}=\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}\,\mathcal{D}\sigma\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}S_{0}}$ to denote the interacting and free partition functions, respectively. We denote iterated integrals over the Euclidean sphere as $\int_{1,2,\cdots,n}\equiv\int_{\{n\}}\equiv\int_{{\mathrm{S}}^{D}}\!\mathrm{d}^{D}z_{1}\,\sqrt{g(z_{1})}\,\mathrm{d}^{D}z_{2}\,\sqrt{g(z_{2})}\cdots$ . Finally, we will use $\langle\,\cdot\,\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ to denote the connected correlation functions in the interacting theory, while we use $\langle\,\cdot\,\rangle$ to denote expectation values taken in the free theory.

We will first derive the leading order long-distance behavior of the propagators (5.11) and (5.12) in §5.1. We then analyze the self-energy $\Pi_{\sigma}(J)$ in the noncompact $\beta\to 0$ and ultracompact $\beta\to\infty$ limits in §5.2 and §5.3, respectively.

### 5.1 Leading-Order Corrections

We begin by considering the one-point functions $\langle\sigma(x)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ and $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ . Clearly, $\langle\sigma(x)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ always vanishes due to the $\sigma\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\sigma$ symmetry of the action. However, there is no such symmetry that prevents a potential for $\varphi$ , or equivalently an expectation value for $\mathcal{V}(x)$ , from being quantum-mechanically generated. So, even absent an explicit classical potential for $\varphi$ with $g_{\varphi}=0$ we should expect that the vertex operator will acquire a vacuum expectation value because, as depicted in Figure 2, there will be a bias in where $\varphi$ prefers to sit induced by fluctuations of $\sigma$ .

The interacting vertex one-point function

$$ \langle\mathcal{V}(x)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}=\frac{1}{\mathcal{Z}}\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}\,\mathcal{D}\sigma\,\mathcal{V}(x)\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}S_{\textsc{e}}} $$

may be computed by expanding out the path integral in powers of the interaction (5.1) and counterterm actions (5.4), though for presentation purposes we will ignore the counterterm action and instead add those contributions in explicitly by hand. Since $S_{\mathrm{int}}$ has no charge neutral interactions, expectation values involving any odd power of $S_{\mathrm{int}}$ alone vanishes, $\langle S_{{\mathrm{int}}}^{2k+1}\rangle=0$ for $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle\geq 0}$ . The “disconnected” part of the path integral can then be written as

$$ \displaystyle\frac{\mathcal{Z}_{0}}{\mathcal{Z}} \displaystyle=\left[\frac{1}{\mathcal{Z}_{0}}\int\!\mathcal{D}\varphi_{\mathrm{c}}\,\mathcal{D}\sigma\,{{\mathrm{e}}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}S_{0}}\left[1-S_{\mathrm{int}}+\tfrac{1}{2}S^{2}_{\mathrm{int}}+\tfrac{1}{4!}S^{4}_{\mathrm{int}}\cdots\right]\right]^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1} \displaystyle=1-\tfrac{1}{2}\langle S_{\mathrm{int}}^{2}\rangle+\tfrac{1}{4}\langle S_{\mathrm{int}}^{2}\rangle^{2}-\tfrac{1}{4!}\langle S_{\mathrm{int}}^{4}\rangle+\cdots\,. $$

