## Appendix A Formulary

In this Appendix, we present the definitions of the various special functions and useful formulae commonly used throughout the main text.

Some of our expressions will contain large ratios and products of gamma functions so it will be convenient to adopt the common shorthand

$$ \Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a, b, c, \cdots$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$d, e, f, \cdots$}}\right]=\frac{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)\Gamma(c)\cdots}{\Gamma(d)\Gamma(e)\Gamma(f)\cdots}\quad\text{and}\quad\Gamma[a,b,c,\cdots]=\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)\Gamma(c)\cdots\,. $$

The $n$ ’th harmonic number $H_{n}\equiv\sum_{k=1}^{n}1/k$ for $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle>0}$ can be analytically continued for arbitrary arguments by the digamma function $\psi(z)$ ,

$$ H_{z}=\psi(z-1)-\gamma_{\textsc{e}}\,, $$

where $\psi(z)=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}z}\log\Gamma(z)$ and $\gamma_{\textsc{e}}\equiv{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\psi(1)\approx={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}0.577216\ldots$ is the Euler-Mascheroni constant.

The Gaussian or ordinary hypergeometric function is defined in the disk $|z|<1$ by the series

$$ {}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;c;z)\equiv{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a, \,\, b$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$c$}}\,\bigg|\,z\,\right]=\frac{\Gamma(c)}{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{\Gamma(a+n)\Gamma(b+n)}{\Gamma(c+n)}\frac{z^{n}}{n!}\,. $$

It obeys the following connection formula when $a-b\notin\mathbb{Z}$ ,

$$ \displaystyle{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a, \,\, b$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$c$}}\,\bigg|\,1+\frac{1}{\zeta}\,\right] \displaystyle=\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$c, b-a$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$b, c-a$}}\right]\left({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\frac{1}{\zeta}\right)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}a}\!\!{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a, \,\, c-b$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$1+a-b$}}\,\bigg|\,{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\zeta\,\right]+(a\leftrightarrow b)\,, $$

which can be used to compute the discontinuity of functions involving (A.3) across its branch cut along $z\in[1,\infty)$ . Similarly, the generalized hypergeometric function ${}_{3}F_{2}(a,b,c;d,e;z)$ can be defined inside the unit disk $|z|<1$ ,

$$ {}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a\,,\,b\,,\,c$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$d\,,\,e$}}\,\bigg|\,z\,\right]\equiv\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$d\,,\,e$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$a\,,\,b,\,\,c$}}\right]\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$a+n\,,\,b+n\,,\,c+n$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$d+n\,,\,e+n$}}\right]\frac{z^{n}}{n!}\,. $$

This generalized hypergeometric function can be related to derivatives of ${}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;c,z)$ with respect to its parameters. For instance, the identity

$$ {}_{2}F_{1}(a,b;c;z)=1+\frac{ab}{c}\,z\,{}_{3}F_{2}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$1,\, 1,\, b + 1$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2,\, c+1$}}\,\bigg|\,z\,\right]+\mathcal{O}\big(a^{2}\big) $$

will be particularly useful.

The Gegenbauer- $C$ function [75] is defined as the solution to the differential equation

$$ \left[(\xi^{2}-1)\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}}{\mathrm{d}\xi^{2}}+(2J+1)\,\xi\,\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}\xi}-J(J+2\alpha)\right]\!C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)=0\, $$

that is regular around $\xi=1$ . It can be expressed in terms of hypergeometric function

$$ C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\equiv\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(J+1)\Gamma(2\alpha)}\,{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$\minus J, \,\, J+2 \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha+ \tfrac{1}{2}$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{1-\xi}{2}\,\right]\,. $$

These functions obey the useful recursion relation

$$ (J+\alpha)C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)=\alpha\!\left[C_{J}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)-C_{J-2}^{\alpha+1}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\xi)\right], $$

as do any appropriately normalized solution to (A.7). They also reduce to the Gegenbauer polynomials for non-negative integer $J$ . As $|\xi|\to\infty$ , they behave as

$$ C_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\sim\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha\,,\,J+1$}}\right](2\hskip 0.5pt\xi)^{J}+\Gamma\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+2 \alpha\,,\, -(J+\alpha)$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$\alpha\,,\,\minus J\,,\, J+1$}}\right](2\xi)^{-(J+2\alpha)}\,, $$

and thus contain both of (A.7)’s asymptotic behaviors when $J\notin\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle>0}$ . At the endpoints of the interval $\xi\in[{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1,1]$ , they simplify to

$$ C_{J}^{\alpha}(1)=\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(J+1)\Gamma(2\alpha)}\qquad\text{and}\qquad C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)=\frac{\cos\pi(J+\alpha)}{\cos\pi\alpha}\frac{\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(J+1)\Gamma(2\alpha)}\,, $$

which both grow as $J^{2\alpha-1}$ for positive real $J$ , though $C_{J}^{\alpha}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}1)$ grows exponentially as ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J\to\pm\infty$ .

The other linearly independent solution to (A.7) is the Gegenbauer- $Q$ function, given by

$$ Q_{J}^{\alpha}(\xi)\equiv\frac{2^{1-J-2\alpha}\pi\Gamma(J+2\alpha)}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(J+\alpha+1)}(\xi-1)^{-J-2\alpha}{}_{2}F_{1}\!\left[\genfrac{.}{.}{-1.0pt}{0}{\raisebox{-1pt}{$J+\alpha+ \frac{1}{2}, \,\, J+2 \alpha$}}{\raisebox{1pt}{$2 J + 2 \alpha+ 1$}}\,\bigg|\,\frac{2}{1-\xi}\,\right]\,. $$

Crucially, this solution decays like $\xi^{-J-2\alpha}$ as $|\xi|\to\infty$ , a fact that allows the Lorentzian inversion formulae (2.13) and (2.14) to work.

## Appendix B Interacting Vertex Two-Point Function

In Section 5, we computed the long-distance behavior of $\sigma$ ’s two-point function $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ by first explicitly expanding the path integral out order-by-order in interactions and then identifying the infinite class of contributions relevant to this limit. There, it was not possible to identify a class of one-particle-irreducible diagrams that could be chained together and readily summed, as our interactions were mediated by vertex operators that do not Wick factorize like free Gaussian fields. However, we argued that at long distances certain higher-order corrections could be well-approximated as an effective chain of diagrams, and so that it was possible to Dyson resum the leading-order correction to $\sigma$ ’s two-point function to determine its corrected long-distance behavior. In this Appendix, we do the same for the connected vertex two-point function $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ and determine how interactions with the heavy scalar $\sigma$ affect its long-distance behavior.

For the sake of brevity, we will ignore the counterterm interactions in intermediate steps and instead only include them in final expressions. To leading order, the vertex two-point function is

$$ \langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle_{\rm c}=\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle+\tfrac{1}{2}\big[\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)S_{\rm int}^{2}\rangle-\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle\langle S_{\rm int}^{2}\rangle\big]-\big|\langle\mathcal{V}(x)S_{\mathrm{int}}\rangle\big|^{2}+\cdots\,, $$

or, by using (4.25),

$$ \displaystyle\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle_{\rm c}=\mathcal{G}(x,y)+\tfrac{1}{2}|g|^{2}\int_{\{2\}}\!(G^{\sigma}_{12})^{2}\!\left[\frac{\mathcal{G}_{xy}\mathcal{G}_{x1}\mathcal{G}_{12}\mathcal{G}_{2y}}{\mathcal{G}_{x2}\mathcal{G}_{1y}}-\mathcal{G}_{xy}\mathcal{G}_{12}\right] \displaystyle+\big|g_{\varphi}+\tfrac{1}{2}g\hskip 0.5ptG^{\sigma}(1)\big|^{2}\int_{\{2\}}\!\left[\frac{\mathcal{G}_{xy}\mathcal{G}_{x1}\mathcal{G}_{12}\mathcal{G}_{2y}}{\mathcal{G}_{x2}\mathcal{G}_{1y}}-\mathcal{G}_{xy}\mathcal{G}_{12}\right]-|g_{\varphi}+\tfrac{1}{2}g\hskip 0.5ptG^{\sigma}(1)|^{2}[\mathcal{G}]_{0}^{2}+\cdots\,, $$

where we have isolated terms related to the one-point function (5.16) on the second line. Even at leading order in perturbation theory, we can see the trouble caused by the fact that free vertex correlators do not Wick factorize since the “disconnected” contribution in $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)S_{\mathrm{int}}^{2}\rangle$ is no longer canceled by $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle\langle S_{\mathrm{int}}^{2}\rangle$ . However, if we are interested in the behavior of this correction at long distances, or analogously around $J\approx{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ , we can follow the discussion of Section 5 and analytically continue (B.2) into an in-in contribution in which the integration variables $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$ span the entire Poincaré patch.

In the long-distance limit $|\xi_{xy}|\to\infty$ , there are two relevant regions of integration. The first is when $z_{1}$ and $z_{2}$ are close to one another yet far from $x$ and $y$ , such that $|\xi_{12}|\ll|\xi_{x1}|,|\xi_{2y}|,\ldots,|\xi_{xy}|$ . In this region, the vertex four-point function factorizes into two disconnected pieces,

