where the $\cdots$ denote contributions from other regions of integration. In momentum space, this contribution strongly diverges as we send $J\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ ,

$$ [\mathcal{V}\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}]\supset[\mathcal{G}]_{J}^{n+1}\left[\tfrac{1}{2}|g|^{2}[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}-J(J+2\alpha)\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}-\delta_{m_{\varphi}}\right]^{\!\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5ptn}, $$

where we expect it to dominate over the regions of integration we have ignored.

To summarize, we have identified contributions at each order in $g$ that become equally important and diverge as $J\to{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ . Diagrammatically, they form a geometric series of bubble diagrams $[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}$ connected by vertex propagators that can be straightforwardly summed. We can therefore approximate the self-energy $\Pi_{\varphi}(J)$ in (5.12) at leading order in perturbation theory around $J\approx{\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ as

$$ \Pi_{\varphi}(J)=\tfrac{1}{2}|g|^{2}[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}-J(J+2\alpha)\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}-\delta_{m_{\varphi}}+\cdots $$

where the bubble diagram $[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}$ can be computed by applying the inversion formula (2.14) to the product $G^{\sigma}(\zeta)G^{\sigma}(\zeta)$ . This diagram has been studied in detail in [37, 39]. It diverges as $[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}\sim(8\pi^{2}\epsilon)^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}$ as $\alpha=\frac{1}{2}(3-\epsilon)\to\frac{3}{2}$ and has poles at $J=-2\Delta_{\sigma}-2k$ , $J=-\Delta_{\sigma}-\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}-2k=-2\alpha-2k$ , and $J=-2\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}-2k$ for all non-negative integers $k\in\mathbb{Z}_{\scriptscriptstyle\geq 0}$ . It is thus convenient to define the regular part of this diagram as $[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{J}=\rho_{\sigma}(J)+1/(8\pi^{2}\epsilon)$ and write

$$ \Pi_{\varphi}(J)=\frac{1}{2}|g|^{2}\rho_{\sigma}(J)+\frac{|g|^{2}}{16\pi^{2}\epsilon}-J(J+2\alpha)\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}-\delta_{m_{\varphi}}\!\hskip 0.5pt\hskip 0.5pt+\cdots\,. $$

Unless $\beta$ is tuned to be an odd integer, the bubble is completely regular at the “free-field pole” $J={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ and, since $\Delta_{\sigma}$ and $\bar{\Delta}_{\sigma}$ are complex conjugates, $\rho_{\sigma}(J)$ is also real along the ${\mathrm{Re}}\,J$ axis.

As discussed in Section 5, the asymptotic behavior of the corrected two-point function is determined by the zero $J_{*}$ of

$$ [\mathcal{G}]_{J_{*}}^{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}1}-\frac{1}{2}|g|^{2}\rho_{\sigma}(J_{*})-\frac{|g|^{2}}{16\pi^{2}\epsilon}+\delta_{m_{\varphi}}=0 $$

closest to the ${\mathrm{Im}}\,J$ -axis. We will choose our counterterms such that this zero is at $J_{*}={\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta$ and $\Pi_{\varphi}^{\prime}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta)=0$ ,

$$ \begin{aligned} \delta_{m_{\varphi}}&=\tfrac{1}{2}|g|^{2}[G^{\sigma}G^{\sigma}]_{{\scalebox{0.6}[0.85]{$-$}}\beta}+\beta(2\alpha-\beta)\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}\,,\\
\delta_{Z_{\varphi}}&=\tfrac{1}{4}|g|^{2}\rho^{\prime}_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta)/(\alpha-\beta)\end{aligned}\,. $$

so that $\beta$ is defined to measure the connected vertex two-point function asymptotic decay such that $\langle\mathcal{V}(x)\mathcal{V}^{\dagger}(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}\sim({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}2/\xi)^{\beta}+\cdots$ as $|\xi|\to\infty$ . The self-energy is nontrivial

$$ \Pi_{\varphi}(J)=\frac{1}{2}|g|^{2}\left[\rho_{\sigma}(J)-\rho_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta)-\frac{(J+\beta)(J+2\alpha-\beta)}{2(\alpha-\beta)}\rho^{\prime}_{\sigma}({\scalebox{0.8}[1.0]{$-$}}\beta)\right]\,, $$

and affects the locations and residues of the other poles of $[\mathcal{G}]_{J}$ , and thus changes the subleading behavior of the vertex propagator, but unlike $\langle\sigma(x)\sigma(y)\rangle_{\mathrm{c}}$ the dominant asymptotic behavior is unaffected by the interactions, as their effect can be completely absorbed by our counterterms.

